  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0219 database is designed to manage the operations of a fuel station, encompassing various aspects such as fuel types, suppliers, customers, employees, fuel pumps, fuel tanks, transactions, payments, tank refills, and maintenance records. The Fuel_type table stores information about the different types of fuel available, including a unique identifier, the type name, and the supplier ID. The Supplier table contains details about the fuel suppliers, including their ID, name, contact number, email, and address. The Fuel_pump table represents the fuel pumps at the station, with attributes such as pump ID, location, capacity, fuel type, status, and the ID of the fuel type it dispenses. The Customer table stores customer information, including their ID, name, contact number, email, and address. The Employee table contains details about the station's employees, including their ID, name, contact number, email, and position. The Fuel_Tank table represents the fuel tanks at the station, with attributes such as tank ID, location, capacity, fuel type, and the current level of fuel. The Transaction table stores records of fuel transactions, including the transaction ID, date and time, fuel amount, payment method, customer ID, pump ID, fuel type ID, and the employee ID of the attendant. The Payment table contains information about the payments made, including the payment ID, amount, payment method, date and time, and the transaction ID it relates to. The Tank_Refill table stores records of fuel refills, including the refill ID, date and time, refill amount, tank ID, supplier ID, and fuel type ID. The Maintenance_Record table stores maintenance records for the fuel pumps, including the maintenance ID, date and time, description, cost, pump ID, employee ID, and supplier ID. Each table has a primary key to uniquely identify each record, and foreign keys are used to establish relationships between tables, such as the Fuel_type ID in the Fuel_pump and Tank_Refill tables referencing the Fuel_type table, and the Supplier_ID in the Fuel_type and Tank_Refill tables referencing the Supplier table. Additionally, some tables have unique constraints to prevent duplicate values, and indexes are likely created to improve query performance.",
  "gold_question": "Fuel Pump Management System \n\n1) Background / Context\n\nThe Fuel Pump Management System aims to efficiently manage operations at fuel stations by tracking interactions between various elements such as fuel pumps, transactions, customers, employees, fuel tanks, payments, and maintenance activities. The system development is phased, each phase enhancing the capability of the overall system, ultimately leading to a comprehensive management database.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- To streamline the management and tracking of transactions and interactions within fuel stations.\n- To ensure data consistency and integrity across all operations related to fuel station management.\n- To provide detailed tracking and reporting of fuel usage and maintenance activities.\n- To enhance decision-making through detailed operational insights.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Fuel Station Owners/Managers: Oversee and manage overall station operations.\n- Employees: Conduct transactions and ensure smooth running of station operations.\n- Customers: Interact with the system through transactions.\n- Suppliers: Provide various types of fuel and auxiliary services.\n- Maintenance Staff: Deal with upkeep and repair operations.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Fuel Pump Management: Connects pumps to specific fuel tanks and tracks usage based on fuel type.\n- Customer Transactions: Logs each transaction to a specific customer and records payment details.\n- Employee Management: Associates employees with each transaction and maintenance activity.\n- Payment Processing: Manages payment details for each transaction and ensures proper linkage.\n- Fuel Type Management: Associates fuel types with both pumps and tanks, managing supply through suppliers.\n- Maintenance Tracking: Records maintenance on pumps and associates related costs with suppliers.\n- Supplier Management: Logs supplier details and correlates them with provided fuel types.\n- Tank Refill Management: Records each refill, specifying the supplier, fuel type, and associated tank.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Data Consistency: Ensure all entries in the database maintain integrity across relationships and transactions.\n- Scalability: System should accommodate increasing volumes of data as operations grow.\n- Security: Protect sensitive data related to customers, transactions, and payments.\n- Usability: Interface should be user-friendly to facilitate ease of use by all stakeholders.\n- Reliability: Ensure system uptime and minimal disruption to operations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0219.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0219\nCREATE TABLE `fuel_type` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `fuel_type_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `supplier` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `fuel_pump` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `capacity` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `fuel_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `fuel_pump_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`fuel_type_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_type` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `customer` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employee` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `fuel_tank` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `capacity` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `fuel_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `current_level` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `fuel_tank_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`fuel_type_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_type` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `transaction` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `date_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `fuel_amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_method` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `pump_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `fuel_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `transaction_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `transaction_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`pump_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_pump` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `transaction_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`fuel_type_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_type` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `transaction_ibfk_4` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payment` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_method` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `date_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `transaction_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`transaction_id`) REFERENCES `transaction` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tank_refill` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `date_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `refill_amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `tank_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `fuel_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `tank_refill_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`tank_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_tank` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `tank_refill_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `tank_refill_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`fuel_type_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_type` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance_record` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `date_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `cost` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `pump_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`pump_id`) REFERENCES `fuel_pump` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `employee` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_record_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
